\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We organize results around four analyses of increasing specificity: the binary ambiguity effect (C1), the type risk gradient (C2), the aggregate layer effect (C3), and the model$\times$type interaction (C4). C1 and C2 constitute our primary claims; C3 is inconclusive; C4 is exploratory but provides the model$\times$type interaction analysis.

\subsection{Binary Ambiguity Effect (C1)}

Across all five models and six types, ambiguous clauses increase violation rates by 34.0 pp (41.0\% vs.\ 6.9\%, OR $= 15.1$, 95\% CI $[10.78, 21.17]$, $p < 10^{-105}$). All six types individually produce significant increases (all $p < 10^{-5}$), and all five models show large effects ($+25.7$ to $+39.0$ pp, all $p < 10^{-17}$). Perturbation controls confirm specificity to semantic ambiguity ($n = 300$, $\Delta_{\text{perturb}} = -0.67$\,pp, CI containing zero). Clause identity explains 22.4\% of variance versus 1.0\% for model identity. This decomposes into 0.4\% between types and 22.0\% within types, indicating that individual clause wording---not type membership---drives the bulk of clause-level variance. A replication on two additional model families (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}) confirms the effect across seven models ($\Delta = +20.0$ to $+33.6$\,pp, both $p < 10^{-6}$); excluding incompleteness, it remains large ($\Delta = 31.0$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sans_incompleteness}).

\subsection{Type Risk Gradient (C2)}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
