\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_forest_plot.pdf}
\caption{Per-type effect sizes ($\Delta$pp, ambiguous minus unambiguous) with Newcombe 95\% CIs. All types produce significant increases. Types form a risk gradient---mid-range equivalence cluster, incompleteness as upper bound, coreferential as lower bound---partly reflecting an information-addition confound for incompleteness.}
\label{fig:forest_plot}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Layer Effect (C3)}

Specification-layer types trend higher than linguistic-layer types (44.0\% vs.\ 37.9\%, $p = 0.017$), but C3 is inconclusive: it does not survive Holm--Bonferroni correction, and controlling for token delta reverses the direction. Cross-layer type overlap (conditional precedence 36.8\% vs.\ scopal 39.4\%) further undermines the distinction. The null C3 result does not diminish the taxonomy's operational value: type-level distinctions (C2) hold independently of layer grouping, and the layer distinction retains value as a theoretical organizing principle even without a measurable effect-size gap.

\subsection{Model $\times$ Type Interaction (C4) \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:c4}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Violation rates (\%) by model and ambiguity type under ambiguous conditions ($n = 2{,}997$). Authorization scope varies 52 pp across models (Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), while incompleteness remains uniformly high (46--62\%).}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20, 2976) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; $\mathrm{df} = (5{-}1) \times (6{-}1) = 20$; 2.9\% of variance; confirmed by logistic regression likelihood ratio test: $\chi^2(20) = 47.20$, $p = 5.5 \times 10^{-4}$) concentrates in specific type--model combinations.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three suggestive patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{most model-differentiating type} (52 pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), though this type has the lowest judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$; \S\ref{sec:limitations}), so absolute rates carry more uncertainty than for other types; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{consistent vulnerability} (46--62\%, 16 pp range); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant type} (34--40\%, 6 pp range). The authorization scope ranking (Claude $<$ Qwen3 $<$ GPT-4.1) is stable within the GPT-5.4 judge subset (bootstrap ranking preservation: 99.5\%, $B = 10{,}000$; pairwise orderings $\geq$99.5\%), controlling for judge identity. The 48\,pp within-judge range far exceeds measurement noise, but given the low cross-judge $\kappa$, we characterize this pattern as suggestive rather than definitive. (The low $\kappa$ partly reflects the prevalence paradox: high base-rate agreement compresses the $\kappa$ denominator even when raw agreement exceeds 80\%; prevalence-adjusted $\kappa$ would be substantially higher; cf.\ the analogous gap in failure-mode coding, \S\ref{sec:limitations}.) Excluding authorization scope, the residual interaction vanishes ($p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}), confirming that the C4 interaction concentrates in this single type. Given the low inter-judge agreement on authorization scope ($\kappa = 0.385$), the 52\,pp range should be treated as an upper bound with substantial measurement uncertainty rather than a precise estimate.

