\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Differential nocuity.}
Building on \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}'s nocuous/innocuous distinction, our results support a \emph{differential nocuity} framework \citep{willis_nocuous_2008}: risk depends on ambiguity type (base vulnerability), safety training (selective mitigation), and disambiguation channel availability. Types with explicit decision points (e.g., authorization scope) appear more amenable to both safety training and prompted intervention than gap-filling types (e.g., incompleteness), where mitigation attempts can backfire (see below).

\paragraph{Prompt-based mitigation.}
Building on the five-model type-specific mitigation study ($-$8.5\,pp pooled; Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}), we test whether disambiguation can be prompted at finer granularity. We evaluate generic awareness prompting (``the policy may contain ambiguous clauses; ask for clarification'') and type-specific prompting (targeting incompleteness, authorization scope, and lexical ambiguity individually) on GPT-5.4 with matched-pair McNemar tests ($n = 50$ per type). Generic prompting produces no overall reduction ($\Delta = +1.3$\,pp, $p > 0.5$). Type-specific results are asymmetric: authorization scope shows a marginal reduction ($-12$\,pp, $p = 0.070$), lexical ambiguity is unchanged ($-2$\,pp), and incompleteness \emph{worsens} ($+14$\,pp, $p = 0.118$; non-significant trend)---suggesting that prompting agents to attend to missing information may trigger confabulation rather than caution. The five-model study in Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation} uses a different prompt formulation and unpaired design, yielding directionally different results; mitigation outcomes appear sensitive to prompt wording and experimental design, reinforcing that no simple prompt-level fix reliably addresses structural policy ambiguity.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
A proof-of-concept classifier (Appendix~\ref{app:detection}) achieves F1\,=\,0.81 for binary ambiguity detection but only 0.34 for incompleteness---the highest-risk type is the hardest to detect. With agents exhibiting disambiguation neglect in $>$89\% of cases, type-aware auditing and externalized detection \citep{chantree_2006,ferrari_ambiguity_2019} are necessary for policy-governed deployments.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Scope.} We evaluate seven API-accessible models from six providers (Appendix~\ref{app:model_expansion}); smaller open-source models (7B--70B) may exhibit different vulnerability patterns. $\tau^2$-bench covers retail and airline customer service only---effects in other domains remain untested, though within-study cross-domain consistency is encouraging. Our matched-pair design requires synthetic ambiguity construction, which may not capture the full distribution of naturally occurring ambiguities; realism validation (\S\ref{sec:method}) confirms lexical comparability but cannot rule out distributional gaps. Coreferential ambiguity was excluded from mitigation and replication experiments due to benchmark-specific structural constraints (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}).

\paragraph{Measurement.} Each model is evaluated by a single cross-judge, confounding judge identity with model identity; within-judge-group analyses confirm C1 individually (both $p < 0.001$) but not C3 (Appendix~\ref{app:judge_group_ranking}), and cross-model type ranking concordance is only moderate (Appendix~\ref{app:kendall_w}). Per-type reliability varies---authorization scope has the lowest agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$) and per-type human--LLM estimates rest on small cell sizes with wide confidence intervals (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_agreement})---while failure mode coding achieves moderate agreement ($\kappa = 0.52$), with minority categories less reliable. Both judges belong to the OpenAI model family, potentially introducing systematic within-family evaluation bias; cross-family judge evaluation (e.g., Claude judging GPT traces) would strengthen C4 conclusions.

\paragraph{Confounds.} Incompleteness resolution necessarily adds policy content, creating an information asymmetry; our logistic regression controls and coreferential lower-bound analysis mitigate but do not eliminate this confound (\S\ref{sec:results}). Our primary analysis uses a moderate-or-above severity threshold; switching to any-violation yields consistent results, with all four claims remaining significant and concordant type rankings.

\section*{Data and Code Availability}
The 300 clause pairs, evaluation pipeline, analysis scripts, and raw episode traces will be released upon publication at \url{https://github.com/anonymous/policy-ambiguity-safety}.
